    {
      "path": "README.md",
      "name": "README.md",
      "kind": "source",
      "source": "# Web64 Turbo Highway\n\nA compact pseudo-3D driving example for Web64 IDE.\n\n## Controls\n\n- Joystick port 2 or keyboard joystick: steer with left/right.\n- Up accelerates.\n- Down brakes.\n- Fire applies a small turbo push.\n\n## Rendering\n\n- The car is a two-sprite multicolor object. Frames 0-1 steer left, 2-3 are\n  straight, and 4-5 steer right. Its 9-bit X coordinate crosses 255 and drives\n  both sprite bits in `$D010`.\n- The 20x12 map uses 2x2 character blocks for the horizon and roadside.\n- Sixteen projected road rows use scanline-accurate slope glyphs. Each curb\n  moves inside its 8-line character, so row boundaries join smoothly.\n- The offline projection deduplicates those shapes into a road-only 2 KB charset.\n  Runtime updates touch only the sparse feature cells whose route state changed.\n- The raster kernel selects the road charset and applies one global `$D016`\n  fine phase at road entry. It does not spend CPU time scrolling every scanline.\n  Only projected `$D021`/`$D023` stripe colors change at band boundaries.\n- A direct raster IRQ chain animates ten projected surface bands through\n  sixteen phases. Their heights grow from four lines to twenty-three near the\n  car without monopolizing all 128 road scanlines.\n- A 512-step route projection advances almost every frame at full speed. Sparse\n  transition packets cap one frame at six changed rows; no glyph construction\n  or charset copy runs in the game loop.\n- PAL provides 19,656 CPU cycles per frame. The generated transition and IRQ\n  model is bounded to 6,848 cycles,\n  below the requested 3/8-frame ceiling of 7,371 cycles.\n- Collision uses the road row beneath the car, not the car position. Failing to\n  steer onto an arriving bend puts the car on the grass and rapidly sheds speed.\n\nThe editable source assets are in `assets/chars`, `assets/blocks`,\n`assets/maps`, and `assets/sprites`.\n"
    },
